  "kurssLesson14": {
    "id": "lesson14",
    "title": "Lesson 14",
    "subtitle": "müssen, wollen, mögen",
    "intro": "Lesson Fourteen: Modal verbs müssen, wollen and mögen.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Text / Reading",
        "items": [
          "Ich muss lernen, denn ich will vorwärts kommen.",
          "Du musst lernen, denn du willst vorwärts kommen.",
          "Er muss lernen, denn er will vorwärts kommen.",
          "Sie muss lernen, denn sie will vorwärts kommen.",
          "Es muss lernen, denn es will vorwärts kommen.",
          "Wir müssen lernen, denn wir wollen vorwärts kommen.",
          "Ihr müsst lernen, denn ihr wollt vorwärts kommen.",
          "Sie müssen lernen, denn sie wollen vorwärts kommen.",
          "Ich mag die Suppe nicht essen, denn sie mundet mir nicht.",
          "Du magst die Suppe nicht essen, denn sie mundet dir nicht.",
          "Er mag die Suppe nicht essen, denn sie mundet ihm nicht.",
          "Sie mag die Suppe nicht essen, denn sie mundet ihr nicht.",
          "Es mag die Suppe nicht essen, denn sie mundet ihm nicht.",
          "Wir mögen die Suppe nicht essen, denn sie mundet uns nicht.",
          "Ihr mögt die Suppe nicht essen, denn sie mundet euch nicht.",
          "Sie mögen die Suppe nicht essen, denn sie mundet ihnen nicht."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Names",
        "items": [
          "müssen - need / have to do",
          "ich muss - I need / I have to do",
          "du musst - you need / you must do",
          "er muss - he needs / he has to",
          "sie muss - she needs / she has to",
          "es muss - it needs / it has to do",
          "wir müssen - we need / we have to do",
          "ihr müsst - you need / you must",
          "sie müssen — they need / have to",
          "lernen - to learn",
          "vorwärts - forward",
          "vorwärts kommen - to get ahead",
          "wollen - to want",
          "ich will — I want",
          "du willst — you want",
          "er will - he wants",
          "sie will - she wants",
          "es will — it wants",
          "wir wollen - we want",
          "ihr wollt - you want",
          "sie wollen — they want",
          "mögen - want / want / like",
          "ich mag - I want / I like",
          "du magst - you want / you like",
          "er mag - he wants / he likes",
          "sie mag - she wants / she likes",
          "es mag - it wants / it likes",
          "wir mögen - we want",
          "ihr mögt - you want",
          "sie mögen — they like",
          "die Suppe — soup",
          "munden — to taste good",
          "mir — to me",
          "dir — to you",
          "ihm—to him",
          "ihr—for her",
          "uns — mums",
          "euch — jums",
          "ihnen — to them",
          "denn — for / because"
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Grammar",
        "items": [
          {
            "heading": "Modal verbs",
            "text": "The verbs müssen, wollen and mögen are modal verbs."
          },
          {
            "heading": "1st and 3rd person singular",
            "text": "In the present singular, the 1st and 3rd persons are the same.",
            "examples": [
              "ich muss",
              "er muss",
              "ich will",
              "er will",
              "ich mag",
              "er mag"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Müssen",
            "examples": [
              "ich muss",
              "du musst",
              "er/sie/es muss",
              "wir müssen",
              "ihr müsst",
              "sie müssen"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Wollen",
            "examples": [
              "ich will",
              "du willst",
              "er/sie/es will",
              "wir wollen",
              "ihr wollt",
              "sie wollen"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Mögen",
            "examples": [
              "ich mag",
              "du magst",
              "er/sie/es mag",
              "wir mögen",
              "ihr mögt",
              "sie mögen"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Stem vowel change",
            "examples": [
              "müssen: ich muss — wir müssen",
              "wollen: ich will — wir wollen",
              "mögen: ich mag — wir mögen"
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Müssen",
            "text": "Müssen means to need/be needed.",
            "examples": [
              "I must learn. - I have to study."
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Wollen",
            "text": "Wollen means to deliberately want to do something.",
            "examples": [
              "I will come forward. — I want to get ahead."
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Mögen",
            "text": "Mögen expresses a wish or liking.",
            "examples": [
              "Ich mag die Suppe nicht essen. — I don't want to eat soup.",
              "Die Suppe mundet mir nicht. — I don't like soup."
            ]
          },
          {
            "heading": "Important",
            "text": "In English we often say \"I have to study\", \"you have to write\", \"he has to come\". German often uses müssen in such sentences.",
            "examples": [
              "Ich muss lernen.",
              "Du musst den Brief schreiben.",
              "Er muss heute kommen."
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Pronunciation",
        "items": [
          "ß is pronounced like English s.",
          "ß is written in the middle or at the end of a word after a long vowel or diphthong: die Füße, die Straße, ich muss, er muss.",
          "If the other forms have ss, then the ending can be preceded by ß: müssen, ich muss, du musst, ihr müsst.",
          "ö in mögen is pronounced as a clear ö sound.",
          "If g is followed by t, then g sounds closer to the sound of k: du magst, ihr mögt.",
          "Similarly, in some languages g before s sounds closer to k; in German it is pronounced as gs."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Translate",
        "type": "translationCards",
        "cards": [
          {
            "lv": "Who wants to study hard?",
            "de": "Wer will fleißig lernen?"
          },
          {
            "lv": "All students want to study hard.",
            "de": "Alle Schüler wollen fleißig lernen."
          },
          {
            "lv": "Who should come today?",
            "de": "Wer muss heute kommen?"
          },
          {
            "lv": "I have to come today.",
            "de": "Ich muss heute kommen."
          },
          {
            "lv": "You have to write a letter.",
            "de": "Du musst den Brief schreiben."
          },
          {
            "lv": "Who needs to study hard?",
            "de": "Wer muss fleißig lernen?"
          },
          {
            "lv": "Students must study hard.",
            "de": "Die Schüler müssen fleißig lernen."
          },
          {
            "lv": "I don't want to eat soup because I don't like it.",
            "de": "Ich mag die Suppe nicht essen, denn sie mundet mir nicht."
          },
          {
            "lv": "Who wants to eat soup?",
            "de": "Wer will die Suppe essen?"
          },
          {
            "lv": "Elsa wants to eat soup.",
            "de": "Elsa will die Suppe essen."
          },
          {
            "lv": "Who should read the book?",
            "de": "Wer muss das Buch lesen?"
          },
          {
            "lv": "Brother must read a book.",
            "de": "Der Bruder muss das Buch lesen."
          },
          {
            "lv": "I don't want to sing.",
            "de": "Ich mag nicht singen."
          },
          {
            "lv": "Father has to buy a table.",
            "de": "Der Vater muss einen Tisch kaufen."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
